  "about": "<div><div><p>The Electrochemistry and Materials Science and Engineering Lab team&#39;s goal is to do <strong>world-leading and world-changing research</strong> while providing an <strong>positive educational environment that launches meaningful and rewarding careers. </strong>Our work spans engineering, chemistry, materials science and more. Boettcher&#39;s PhD students have gone on to found companies based on the group&#39;s research and discoveries, including <a href=\"https://www.nuwohm.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Nuwohm </a>and<a href=\"https://dycosystems.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"> Dyco Systems</a>. </p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://boettcher.uoregon.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Boettcher lab at University of Oregon</a> transitioned to UC Berkeley and LBNL in January 2024. </p>\n<p>Prof. Boettcher was the founding Director of the<a href=\"http://electrochemistry.uoregon.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"> Oregon Center for Electrochemistry</a> and the accelerated <a href=\"https://electrochemistry.uoregon.edu/masters-internship-program/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MS program in Electrochemical Science and Technology/Engineering</a>We have launched the first phase of the <a href=\"#/outreach\"><strong>Electrochemistry Academy</strong></a> at UC Berkeley through CESET, the <a href=\"#/ceset\">Center for Electrochemical Science, Engineering, and Technology</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Interest in postdoctoral, PhD, visiting scholar, or undergraduate research opportunities should be communicated to the UC email and in consideration for appointments at UCB or LBNL. </strong><strong>Because Prof. Boettcher receives a large volume of requests and inquiries, he may not respond to each one. </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Postdoctoral candidates are encouraged to seek out external fellowship funding for work at UC Berkeley.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Prof. Boettcher’s UC email is <a href=\"mailto:boettcher@berkeley.edu\">boettcher@berkeley.edu</a> and his LBNL email is <a href=\"mailto:sboettcher@lbl.gov\">sboettcher@lbl.gov</a>. His office is Gilman 212.  Prof. Boettcher receives much email. <em>If you email him and do not hear back in three days, and it is important, send a reminder!</em></strong></p>\n\n<h3><strong>Laboratory Principles</strong></h3>\n<p>The Boettcher group strives to create an inclusive, respectful environment to support a diverse community of students, staff, and faculty. We believe in helping every member achieve personal and professional growth to the maximum exent possible. <strong>We welcome and encourage talented and motivated individuals of all racial, cultural, and socioeconomic backgrounds, nationalities, political or religious beliefs, and all sexual orientations, gender identities, disabilities, to join us here in Berkeley in our pursuit of advancing electrochemical science and technology to drive positive impact in our world!</strong></p>\n\n\n</div></div>",
  "research": "<p>The Boettcher electrochemistry and materials engineering laboratory studies, designs, synthesizes, fabricates, and models materials and devices for applications in electrochemical energy storage and solar energy conversion. Our work spans use-inspired basic science to applied science and engineering, and technology transfer through industry collaborations and partnerships.</p><p>We are particularly focused on uncovering the fundamental insights into how ions and electrons transfer at interfaces and using those insights to design and make materials and devices for efficient energy conversion and storage and manufacturing. </p><p>Specific interests include the synthesis and study of heterogeneous electrocatalysts for water oxidation with defined molecular and nanoscale structures, the use of computer simulation and direct electrical measurements to understand semiconductor-electrocatalyst interfaces in photochemistry, the development of alkaline membrane electrolyzers for low-cost scalable hydrogen production, fundamentals and applications of bipolar membranes and electric field driven interfacial ionic reactions like water dissociation and corrosion.</p>",
  "learning": "<div><div><p>The page contains links to useful internet-based materials to help researchers in the multidisciplinary field of electrochemistry succeed.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLyQSN7X0ro2314mKyUiOILaOC2hk6Pc3j\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Calculus-based first year electrostatics from MIT</a> - this core material is essential. Most engineers and chemists need to review/deepen/relearn this material to accelerate their work in electrochemistry and electrochemical technology.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://electrochemistry.uoregon.edu/education/electrochemistry-video-lectures/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Video Lectures</a> for complete and short courses on Fundamental Electrochemistry, Electrochemical Engineering, and other topics from Prof. Boettcher.</p>\n\n</div></div>",
