        "description": "<p>Terms offered: Summer Session D: Second 6 Week Session<br>Electrochemical engineering combines the study of charge transfer at electrode/electrolyte interfaces with the development of practical materials and processes. Electrochemical devices/reactors, their voltage and current distribution, mass-transport, hydrodynamics, geometry, and overall performance in terms of reaction yield, conversion efficiency, and energy efficiency are examined. Electrochemical energy storage (batteries and capacitors), energy conversion (low- and high-temperature fuel cells and electrolyzers), and metal plating and electrosynthesis devices are covered. Fundamental chemistry, physics, and engineering principles that govern device response are emphasized.</p><p><strong>Prerequisites:</strong> CHMENG 186/286 Advanced Electrochemistry Fundamentals</p><p>Visiting summer students sometimes are not able to enroll in 186/286 due to conflicts with timing at the home institution. The content contained in 186/286 is generally available here for self study: <a href=\"https://electrochemistry.uoregon.edu/education/electrochemistry-video-lectures/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">https://electrochemistry.uoregon.edu/education/electrochemistry-video-lectures/</a></p><p><a href=\"assets/documents/electrochemical-engineering-syllabus.pdf\">View course syllabus</a></p>",
